  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's learn the most useful simplification habit in this chapter: make the bases match.\n\nA problem may show 64, 8 and 2 as if they are different. But:\n\n64 = 2^6\n8 = 2^3.\n\nOnce the base is the same, the exponent laws become easy to use.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "concept_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "concept_1": {
      "id": "concept_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Start with a direct rewrite.\n\n64^2 ÷ 8^3\n= (2^6)^2 ÷ (2^3)^3\n= 2^12 ÷ 2^9\n= 2^3\n= 8.\n\nThe arithmetic became an exponent subtraction.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "q_1": {
